  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 946 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low stratus and patchy fog is possible late tonight and early\n Sunday morning. \n\nEvening weather analysis shows high pressure centered over the \narea. This has allowed showers to dwindle and winds to calm. \nThe front that passed through the area this afternoon continues \nto remain just along the Albemarle sound. North of the front \nsome residual moisture remains in place with dew points in the \nupper 60s to low 70s. With the residual moisture and calm winds,\nareas of fog and low stratus are possible tonight into early \nSunday morning. Models have continues to trend with widespread \nfog across the piedmont stretching down into SE VA which could \npossible cause reduced visibility through the early morning \nhours of Sunday. \n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot conditions prevail during the early to middle \nportion of next week with increasing chances for showers and storms \nby late week. \n\nHigh pressure centers offshore Sunday, forcing the remnants of \nthe boundary well south of the local area. Outside of an \nisolated shower or storm, mostly dry weather and partly sunny \nskies are expected across the area. Highs on Sunday warm into \nthe upper 80s to around 90.\n\nA strong 500mb upper level ridge expands eastward over the Ohio \nValley and into the Mid-Atlantic early next week. Warm advection and \ndeep mixing will allow for temperatures to rise into the low to mid \n90s on Monday. The ridge peaks in strength Tuesday into Wednesday \nbefore flattening later in the weak. Tuesday through Thursday will \nsee high temperatures likely in the mid to even upper 90s for many \ninland locations. Max heat indices through the period should \ngenerally average 100-104 due to deep mixing keeping dewpoints in \nthe upper 60s to lower 70s. However, if dewpoints overperform, a \nHeat Advisory may be needed across portions of southeast Virginia \nand northeast North Carolina. \n\nConvective chances remain capped near climatological minimums \n(15-20% or less) through Wednesday due to strong mid level \nsubsidence. A broad upper trough and associated cold front \napproach from the Great Lakes Thursday into Friday, bringing the\nnext meaningful opportunity for showers and thunderstorms along\nwith more seasonable temperatures. AI/machine learning tools \ncontinue to hint at the possibility for severe weather Thursday \ninto Friday as the front approaches the area.",
